'''Meteorite''' is a [[pre-Hardmode]] [[ore]] typically mined from [[Meteorite (biome)|Meteorite crash sites]]<!-- "typically" because it can also be obtained by decrafting Meteorite Bars found in Chests near the Underworld -->. It also spawns in high numbers during a [[Meteor Shower]] and rarely drops from [[Meteor Head]]s. A Meteorite crash site is a [[mini-biome]] guaranteed to spawn after the [[Eater of Worlds]] or [[Brain of Cthulhu]] is defeated for the first time.{{source code ref|name=dodeathevents|v=1.4.4.9|method=Terraria.NPC.DoDeathEvents}} If the boss is defeated between [[Day and night cycle|midnight]] and dawn ({{time|12:00 AM}} – {{time|4:30 AM}}), then the meteor crashes immediately. Otherwise, it will crash at the next midnight ({{time|12:00 AM}}). Meteorite can be mined using a [[Tungsten Pickaxe]] or better (its counterpart, the [[Silver Pickaxe]], does not have enough pickaxe power).

Contact with Meteorite blocks causes the player to be inflicted with the [[Burning]] [[debuff]], unless the player has the [[Obsidian Skull]] accessory (or its upgrades) equipped, or is using an [[Obsidian Skin Potion]].

== Crafting ==
=== Used in ===
{{recipes|ingredient=Meteorite|expectedrows=2}}

== Notes ==
* [[Water]] and [[honey]] will not prevent damage from a Meteorite block.
* Meteorite cannot be [[Explosion-proof objects|broken by explosives]] before Hardmode.

== Tips ==
* Turning Meteorite into [[Meteorite Bar]]s and selling them is {{percent|{{sell expr|raw=y|Meteorite Bar|/(|Meteorite|*3 <!-- 3 Meteorite make 1 Meteorite Bar -->)}} -1 round 2}} more profitable than selling an equivalent amount of raw Meteorite itself.
* If a player places at least 75 pieces of Meteorite, they can create an artificial Meteorite biome, where all enemy spawns are replaced with Meteor Heads.
* While water and honey will not prevent the damage from Meteorite blocks, they will cancel the [[On Fire!]] debuff that Meteor Heads inflict.

== Trivia ==
* Meteorite, [[Hellstone]], and [[Luminite]] are the only ores that do not have "ore" in their names.
* In real life, {{wikipedia|meteoric iron}} was used as the main source of iron metal in ancient cultures.
* Meteorite emits a small amount of light when placed. It also periodically emits bright particles.

