[[File:Falling Star Terraria.png|thumb|A star falling from the sky.]]

The '''Fallen Star''' is an item that randomly falls from the sky at [[night]] and disappears at dawn ({{time|4:30 AM}}). They can be used to craft [[Mana Crystal]]s, which permanently increase a player's [[mana]] capacity by 20 points each. They are used in a variety of crafting recipes and as [[ammunition]] for the [[Star Cannon]] and [[Super Star Shooter]]. Fallen Stars can be stored in [[ammo slot]]s.

Fallen Stars will spawn randomly throughout nighttime and their spawn rate is determined by two factors: the [[world size]] and a multiplier randomly set at nightfall (''see [[#Notes|Notes]] for details'').

Fallen Stars emit light, making them easy to spot at night. They cannot be [[placed]] by the player as permanent [[light sources]], but can be combined with a [[Bottle]] to craft the [[Star in a Bottle]]. They disappear at dawn if outside of the player's [[inventory]] or a [[storage item]], even if dropped by the player.

If a Fallen Star strikes an [[enemy]] or [[critter]] while falling, it will deal 1,000 base damage to the struck entity.{{source code ref|name=starspawner|v=1.4.4.9|method=Terraria.Projectile.AI_148_StarSpawner}} Fallen Stars cannot damage players or [[NPCs]] unless playing in a world with the [[Remix]] [[special world seed]] enabled. On these worlds, Fallen Stars that fall from the sky do not drop as an item on impact, and can only be obtained from [[pots]] or [[slimes]] as their [[bonus drop]]. Fallen Stars will deal 2,000 / {{expert|4,000}} / {{master|6,000}} to players on these worlds.

== Crafting ==
=== Used in ===
{{recipes|ingredient=Fallen Star|expectedrows=16}}

== Notes ==
* The default Fallen Star multiplier is 1. Each night, the multiplier will be randomly increased. There is a {{chance|1/10}} chance ({{chance|1/5}} chance if the [[Celebration Mk 10]] seed is enabled) for a [[Starfall]] to be started, and a random value between 300 and 500 (inclusive) is chosen. If this failed, then there is a {{chance|1/3}} chance ({{chance|1/2}} chance in Celebrationmk10 worlds) for a random value between 100 and 150 (inclusive) to be chosen. The multiplier is then set to 0.01 × chosen value.{{source code ref|v=1.4.5.8|method=Terraria.Star.NightSetup}}
** This equals a 200%–400% increase during Starfalls, and a 0%–50% increase during regular nights.
: Then, the spawn of Fallen Stars will be determined each game tick{{tick}}. The game will use the total width of the world (<code>maxTilesX</code>) and the above multiplier to determine the spawn chance. The formula is:{{source code ref|v=1.4.5.8|method=Terraria.WorldGen.SpawnFallingObjects}}
: <math>\text{Chance}=\frac{10\times\frac{\text{maxTilesX}}{4200}\times\text{multiplier}}{8000}</math>
:* When the multiplier is 1, the spawn chance per tick is {{chance|{{#expr:10*(4200<!--maxTilesX small-->/4200)}}/8000}} for small worlds, {{chance|{{#expr:10*(6400<!--maxTilesX medium-->/4200) round 0}}/8000}} for medium worlds, and {{chance|{{#expr:10*(8400<!--maxTilesX large-->/4200)}}/8000}} for large worlds.
:* Therefore, the average number of Fallen Stars per night (when the multiplier is 1) is 40.5 in a small world, about 61.71 in a medium world, and 81 in a large world.
* Fallen Stars spawn anywhere within the upper 5% of the world, i.e. 60 tiles in small worlds, 90 tiles in medium worlds, and 120 tiles in large worlds.
** Note that here are 40 inacessible tiles above the visible top [[world border]] which are included in this area. Therefore when only counting the accessible area, they can spawn in upper 20 tiles in small worlds, 50 tiles in medium worlds, and 80 tiles in large worlds.
* Fallen Stars have a {{chance|1/15}} chance to spawn within 40 horizontal tiles on each side of the closest player, as long as they have moved or used an item (other than [[kites]]) within the past {{duration|3600 ticks}}, and are not [[Underground]] or below. The Fallen Star's trajectory will be angled towards the player.
** This chance is affected by the closest player's luck.
*** If the [[Remix]] seed is enabled, this chance is instead inversely affected by the closest player's luck.
* [[Moon phase]] and lateral map position have no effect on the frequency of Fallen Stars. Their appearance is at a fixed rate at all hours of every night.
* Unlike most items collected as drops, Fallen Stars fall all over the world at night, even in areas far from the player.
* Fallen Stars will not fall while time is frozen in [[Journey Mode]].

== Tips ==
* Sleeping in a [[Bed]] will fast-forward time, which increases the rate at which stars fall relative to real-world time. This can be exploited to quickly gather stars, by sleeping for most of the night then collecting accumulated stars near the end. This also applies to [[Journey Mode]]'s time acceleration which at 10× allows for very quick Fallen Star farming.
* Traveling a far distance a few minutes prior to dawn will allow the player to collect the Fallen Stars, easily netting a large supply.
* A simple method for harvesting Fallen Stars is to construct long straight sections of ground to run along at night; a [[skybridge]] also works well. Note that diagonal [[platforms]] may not intercept Fallen Stars. When constructing a large skybridge, the platform to catch them should be placed at the bottom of [[Space]].
* It is possible to rapidly hoik Fallen Stars to a desirable location, but the stars must enter the hoik by toggling [[Active Stone Block]]s or [[Actuator|actuated]] blocks, making the setup a bit expensive.
* To pass Fallen Stars to another player during daytime, transfer it using a [[storage item]], e.g. a [[Chest]], since throwing it out of the inventory during the day will make it vanish.
* If the player's inventory is full, Fallen Stars can be moved to an empty ammo slot to free up an inventory slot.
* Fallen Stars will hit [[Floating Island]]s if the island is in the path of the star, so if there is an area where Fallen Stars never seem to fall or rarely do, there is a possibility that a Floating Island is above that area.

== Trivia ==
* If "used" via {{key|use}}, the player will hold the star above their head, and a {{sound|[[Media:Item_4.wav|sound ]]|Item_4.wav|nofallback=y}} will play, similarly to the [[Mana Crystal]]. However, unlike the Mana Crystal, it will neither be consumed nor have any effect on the player. This effect is left over from before [[1.1]], when Fallen Stars were used to restore mana.
* The Fallen Star is fully bright in the dark when placed inside an [[Item Frame]] or an [[Item Flask]].
* There is a projectile related with the spawning of Fallen Stars, with the [[Projectile IDs|ID]] 720.<ref name="starspawner"/> Technically, when a Fallen Star is going to be spawned, this projectile is spawned first, then it creates a Fallen Star projectile.

